      "deepExplanation": "Understanding Garbage Collection helps engineers write predictable, maintainable and performant production code and reason about interview problems without relying on memorized output. Modern JavaScript is standardized by ECMAScript and implemented by engines such as V8, SpiderMonkey and JavaScriptCore. Feature availability should be checked against the application's supported browser/Node.js versions. The engine parses source into internal representations and executes it through an interpreter and/or optimized JIT pipeline. Hot code may be optimized using runtime feedback; assumptions can later be invalidated and deoptimized. In browsers, JavaScript runs inside a browser-provided runtime with DOM and Web APIs. Node.js provides a server-side runtime with Node APIs and an event-driven I/O model. ECMAScript language semantics remain distinct from host APIs. Primitive values and object references have different observable semantics, while the engine's actual memory placement is an implementation detail. Objects are generally managed in heap-managed storage and reclaimed by garbage collection when unreachable. At senior level, connect the language feature to the runtime. JavaScript source is parsed into internal representations, executed in execution contexts, and may be optimized by the engine based on runtime feedback. Objects and closures create references that participate in garbage collection. Browser and Node.js environments add host APIs around the ECMAScript language. For production systems, correctness comes first, followed by observability, performance measurement, security and maintainability. A strong engineer can explain not only what code returns, but why it returns that value and what happens when scale, concurrency, memory pressure or hostile input changes the conditions.",
